{"schema_version":2,"catalogue_version":1,"id":"bco:32-16","provision":"BCO 32-16","book":"bco","book_name":"Book of Church Order","abbreviation":"BCO","reference":"32-16","title":"Section 32-16","supplementary":false,"canonical_url":"https://raymond-rishty.github.io/pca-ga/provisions/bco/32-16/","reader_url":"https://raymond-rishty.github.io/pca-constitution-reader/#bco/32-16","current_text":{"html":"<p>Either party may, for cause, challenge the right of any member to sit in the trial of the case, which question shall be decided by the other members of the court.</p>","text":"Either party may, for cause, challenge the right of any member to sit in the trial of the case, which question shall be decided by the other members of the court."},"source":{"name":"PCA Constitution Reader","revision":"edce8a8dcd9ced63bb585bf922e6a97319ef62e1","edition":"Current Reader text","files":{"bco.js":"c88e552545b73fd9690de620b237895b7ea52a94bed8e95690c116bf76be8ae5","wcf.js":"1ebf9e54223f7fe4e728a3e640c833a435af825d74b5ef3d4b04687685538a0d","wlc.js":"f6977e761ea4ccc93b07fcacfd0391890cb05b42f4317d807fa293c078d9b051","wsc.js":"dd775417f7ef21e3283bacad5e6ef193c41d64d03bd5a2e8fb934ccca8cb8e53","rao.js":"49808bbb0e95abdc3b07506c87dde0d62d087851e869b49c0f1f2424986450ec"}},"input_fingerprint":"94a0b54eec213ad53deb08f9fe4e387f46d90e3373f4cd38647b018d7b192c88","parent_id":"bco:32","children":[],"coverage":{"relationships":"indexed_records","recommendations":{"status":"incomplete","note":"Recommendation and study papers are not comprehensively indexed by provision."},"history":"partial"},"relationship_count":7,"relationships":[{"id":"bco:32-16--ccb_advice:inquiries/ga52_2025__ci21.md","record_id":"ccb_advice:inquiries/ga52_2025__ci21.md","type":"CCB advice","title":"Elevating a censure without a new trial under BCO 36","year":2025,"disposition":"ambiguous; Adopted 8-0-0","authority_weight":"medium","url":"https://raymond-rishty.github.io/pca-ga/inquiries/ga52_2025__ci21.html","raw_url":"https://raw.githubusercontent.com/raymond-rishty/pca-ga/main/inquiries/ga52_2025__ci21.md","evidence_basis":"structured_provision_tag","relevance_status":"unreviewed","occurrences":[{"id":"bco:32-16--ccb_advice:inquiries/ga52_2025__ci21.md:occ-d59f8972bbb8","url":"https://raymond-rishty.github.io/pca-ga/inquiries/ga52_2025__ci21.html","repository_path":"inquiries/ga52_2025__ci21.md","locator":{},"excerpt":"Proposed amendment to BCO 36 providing for censure elevation without a wholly new trial was found to be ambiguous under the BCO.","sources":[],"raw_url":"https://raw.githubusercontent.com/raymond-rishty/pca-ga/main/inquiries/ga52_2025__ci21.md"}]},{"id":"bco:32-16--case:cases/ga49_2022__2021-13.md","record_id":"case:cases/ga49_2022__2021-13.md","type":"Judicial case","title":"Dudt v. Northwest Georgia Presbytery","year":2022,"disposition":"sustained","authority_weight":"high","url":"https://raymond-rishty.github.io/pca-ga/cases/ga49_2022__2021-13.html","raw_url":"https://raw.githubusercontent.com/raymond-rishty/pca-ga/main/cases/ga49_2022__2021-13.md","evidence_basis":"structured_case_metadata","relevance_status":"unreviewed","occurrences":[{"id":"bco:32-16--case:cases/ga49_2022__2021-13.md:occ-2970933ec1e5","url":"https://raymond-rishty.github.io/pca-ga/cases/ga49_2022__2021-13.html","repository_path":"cases/ga49_2022__2021-13.md","locator":{},"excerpt":"","sources":["cases.jsonl:bco_cited_as","cases.jsonl:bco_cited_current","judicial_cases.jsonl:bco_provisions"],"raw_url":"https://raw.githubusercontent.com/raymond-rishty/pca-ga/main/cases/ga49_2022__2021-13.md"}],"metadata":{"topics":["ruling elder discipline","email to congregation","session authority over elders","ordination vows","subjection to brethren","express vs. implied will of session","ninth commandment","burden of proof","suspension from office","notice of appeal","bill of attainder","Talley Resolution","elder dissent","BCO 39-3 appellate standard of review"],"standard_of_review":"mixed","review_standards":["factual_findings","discretion_and_judgment"],"case_numbers":["2021-13"],"body":"SJC","synopsis":"A ruling elder appealed convictions for distributing an SJC decision contrary to Session's will and for allegedly false, divisive statements in an email opposing a pastoral-election proposal. The SJC sustained the appeal, reversed Session and Presbytery in whole, rendered not guilty, removed the indefinite suspension, and restored him to office. Session could not bind conscience without scriptural authority, and no express prohibition or proof of falsity existed. Many other specifications were rejected; a concurrence cautioned that such emails are rarely wise."}},{"id":"bco:32-16--case:cases/ga49_2022__2021-10.md","record_id":"case:cases/ga49_2022__2021-10.md","type":"Judicial case","title":"Williams v. Chesapeake Presbytery","year":2022,"disposition":"denied","authority_weight":"high","url":"https://raymond-rishty.github.io/pca-ga/cases/ga49_2022__2021-10.html","raw_url":"https://raw.githubusercontent.com/raymond-rishty/pca-ga/main/cases/ga49_2022__2021-10.md","evidence_basis":"direct_text","relevance_status":"unreviewed","occurrences":[{"id":"bco:32-16--case:cases/ga49_2022__2021-10.md:occ-f797eedbbe46","url":"https://raymond-rishty.github.io/pca-ga/cases/ga49_2022__2021-10.html","repository_path":"cases/ga49_2022__2021-10.md","locator":{"fragment":null,"line":145},"excerpt":"Moreover, BCO 32-16 establishes the right of an accused to challenge any","sources":["case_markdown_text","cases.jsonl:bco_cited_as","cases.jsonl:bco_cited_current"],"raw_url":"https://raw.githubusercontent.com/raymond-rishty/pca-ga/main/cases/ga49_2022__2021-10.md"}],"metadata":{"topics":["deposition from office","due process","refusal of reasonable indulgence","improper evidence","prejudice","error in judgment and censure","trial procedure","re-hearing by same judges","BCO 32 discipline","case without process"],"case_numbers":["2021-10"],"body":"SJC","synopsis":"TE Williams appealed Chesapeake Presbytery's approval of his deposition from office and suspension from the Sacraments after trial on three charges (failure to manage household, slander, inappropriate behavior). The SJC denied all five specifications of error and upheld the Presbytery's decision in whole."}},{"id":"bco:32-16--case:cases/ga48_2021__2019-03.md","record_id":"case:cases/ga48_2021__2019-03.md","type":"Judicial case","title":"Crouse v. Northwest Georgia Presbytery","year":2021,"disposition":"sustained","authority_weight":"high","url":"https://raymond-rishty.github.io/pca-ga/cases/ga48_2021__2019-03.html","raw_url":"https://raw.githubusercontent.com/raymond-rishty/pca-ga/main/cases/ga48_2021__2019-03.md","evidence_basis":"structured_case_metadata","relevance_status":"unreviewed","occurrences":[{"id":"bco:32-16--case:cases/ga48_2021__2019-03.md:occ-f15690f09da8","url":"https://raymond-rishty.github.io/pca-ga/cases/ga48_2021__2019-03.html","repository_path":"cases/ga48_2021__2019-03.md","locator":{},"excerpt":"","sources":["cases.jsonl:bco_cited_as","cases.jsonl:bco_cited_current","judicial_cases.jsonl:bco_provisions"],"raw_url":"https://raw.githubusercontent.com/raymond-rishty/pca-ga/main/cases/ga48_2021__2019-03.md"}],"metadata":{"topics":["officer nomination process","ruling elder election","session prescreening nominees","mandatory sequence BCO 24-1","divorce and church office","session discretion"],"standard_of_review":"discretion_and_judgment","review_standards":["discretion_and_judgment"],"case_numbers":["2019-3"],"body":"SJC","synopsis":"A serving deacon nominated for ruling elder complained that his session removed his nomination before BCO 24-1 instruction or examination. The SJC held the session erred by adding an unwritten pre-examination 'vetting' removal and that Northwest Georgia Presbytery erred in approving it: BCO 24-1's sequence—instruction and examination before the session determines eligibility—is mandatory. The ruling reserved the session's sound discretion and the treatment of frivolous or clearly disqualified nominees. The complaint was sustained and remanded for adjudication consistent with the decision."}},{"id":"bco:32-16--case:cases/ga32_2004__2002-14.md","record_id":"case:cases/ga32_2004__2002-14.md","type":"Judicial case","title":"Lachman v. Philadelphia Presbytery","year":2004,"disposition":"denied","authority_weight":"high","url":"https://raymond-rishty.github.io/pca-ga/cases/ga32_2004__2002-14.html","raw_url":"https://raw.githubusercontent.com/raymond-rishty/pca-ga/main/cases/ga32_2004__2002-14.md","evidence_basis":"direct_text","relevance_status":"unreviewed","occurrences":[{"id":"bco:32-16--case:cases/ga32_2004__2002-14.md:occ-a2e03927e678","url":"https://raymond-rishty.github.io/pca-ga/cases/ga32_2004__2002-14.html","repository_path":"cases/ga32_2004__2002-14.md","locator":{"fragment":null,"line":177},"excerpt":"Finally, while the BCO does not specifically mention the accused party's right to petition for change of venue, BCO 32-16 alludes to something similar:","sources":["case_markdown_text","cases.jsonl:bco_cited_as","cases.jsonl:bco_cited_current","judicial_cases.jsonl:bco_provisions"],"raw_url":"https://raw.githubusercontent.com/raymond-rishty/pca-ga/main/cases/ga32_2004__2002-14.md"}],"metadata":{"topics":["ruling elder suspension","documentary evidence","witness requirement","Matthew 18 process","ninth commandment","fifth commandment","sixth commandment","change of venue","references to presbytery","fair and impartial trial","session as court of original jurisdiction"],"standard_of_review":"not_stated","case_numbers":["2002-14"],"body":"SJC","synopsis":"A ruling elder convicted by his Session of fifth-, sixth-, and ninth-commandment violations challenged the conviction because the prosecution presented no live witness and relied on his own letters. The SJC denied the appeal and upheld Presbytery's affirmance, holding that the documents, Lachman's acknowledgments of writing them, and defense witnesses supplied sufficient proof; the Session also followed Matthew 18 with patience. A concurrence urged change-of-venue procedures for small-Session bias cases, but noted Lachman never requested a reference or challenged the court before trial."}},{"id":"bco:32-16--case:cases/ga23_1995__1994-08.md","record_id":"case:cases/ga23_1995__1994-08.md","type":"Judicial case","title":"Thomas W. Berry vs. Susquehanna Valley Presbytery","year":1994,"disposition":"affirmed","authority_weight":"high","url":"https://raymond-rishty.github.io/pca-ga/cases/ga23_1995__1994-08.html","raw_url":"https://raw.githubusercontent.com/raymond-rishty/pca-ga/main/cases/ga23_1995__1994-08.md","evidence_basis":"direct_text","relevance_status":"unreviewed","occurrences":[{"id":"bco:32-16--case:cases/ga23_1995__1994-08.md:occ-1bfbcf49a012","url":"https://raymond-rishty.github.io/pca-ga/cases/ga23_1995__1994-08.html","repository_path":"cases/ga23_1995__1994-08.md","locator":{"fragment":null,"line":90},"excerpt":"to sit on the court.\" The Presbytery reasoned correctly that although BCO 32-16 permits either","sources":["case_markdown_text"],"raw_url":"https://raw.githubusercontent.com/raymond-rishty/pca-ga/main/cases/ga23_1995__1994-08.md"},{"id":"bco:32-16--case:cases/ga23_1995__1994-08.md:occ-5219db239284","url":"https://raymond-rishty.github.io/pca-ga/cases/ga23_1995__1994-08.html","repository_path":"cases/ga23_1995__1994-08.md","locator":{"fragment":null,"line":187},"excerpt":"BCO 32-16 states,","sources":["case_markdown_text"],"raw_url":"https://raw.githubusercontent.com/raymond-rishty/pca-ga/main/cases/ga23_1995__1994-08.md"}],"metadata":{"case_numbers":["1994-8"],"body":"SJC/CJB"}},{"id":"bco:32-16--case:cases/ga23_1995__1994-07.md","record_id":"case:cases/ga23_1995__1994-07.md","type":"Judicial case","title":"Wayne E. Wheatley vs. Susquehanna Valley Presbytery","year":1994,"disposition":"affirmed","authority_weight":"high","url":"https://raymond-rishty.github.io/pca-ga/cases/ga23_1995__1994-07.html","r